    "jays": {
      "full_name": "Toronto Blue Jays",
      "league": "MLB",
      "record": "19-25",
      "detail": "4th in AL East",
      "lotl": {
        "label": "What's Going On With the Jays",
        "body": "The Blue Jays keep finding new ways to make a mess of a perfectly ordinary night. <strong>Friday's 3-2 loss in Detroit</strong> was the latest punch to the ribs - a game they once led 2-0 and then watched slip away when Spencer Torkelson dropped a two-out, ninth-inning single into the grass. That's the sort of defeat that lingers, because it deepens a season already tilting the wrong way: 19-25, fourth in the AL East, 10.5 games back, and carrying a one-game losing streak that feels heavier than that. The offense has flashes - Amed Rosario's lineup can still scratch - but the Jays keep living on the edge, and lately the edge has been a cliff. Trey Yesavage gave them six innings and six strikeouts, but the bullpen couldn't hold the door, and that has become too familiar a scene. There's no mystery here. They need cleaner late innings, more timely hits, and a little backbone. <strong>What matters next is the matinee in Detroit today at 12:10 p.m. ET - because if Toronto can't answer fast, this trip will start looking like another long, dispiriting slog.</strong>",
        "updated": "Updated May 16, 2026"
      },
